I should really clarify this.  A quick statement of my world:

I am 35 years old.  I have a full-time job, a live-in girlfriend, 
two cats, and a (reasonably) active social life; I'm doing Spork 
in what spare time I actually have available, and that involves not 
_just_ sitting and adding to a patch, but maintaining/running the 
website and online server as well as the bot that sits in-channel 
and announces deaths.

As such, I am _extremely_ harsh with what I think is worth my time.  
That is because I don't have very much of it, and I have a _lot_ of ideas 
that I'd like to add (the website's list is extremely outdated).  To that
end, things that make no effectual change in gameplay are very likely to
be filed in "not worth it"; enhancements such as "socketed weapons", for
example, are absolutely not worth it because what they add to gameplay
doesn't fit in anywhere.  There's not much room between a +7 longsword 
and a +7 Fire Brand right now; the former is good enough to ascend with, 
so if you want something better than the former, you'd be better off 
spending time saccing for artifacts than digging for gems; especially
when the artifact is significantly more powerful than a jeweled weapon.

> many of the times when he has put off an idea was because 1. it really
> doesnt change much and really is not worth the amount of time and
> energy it would take to implement it (hi2u judgement call?)

Note that even one-liners can fall under this.  Just because it only
needs one line of code to change something doesn't mean it takes a similar
amount of time to verify the effects of that change behave reasonably
as-intended.

